{
  "requirement": "A `{req}` definition MUST use an opening fence of at least three colons immediately followed by `{req} <name>`. A closing fence MUST have at least as many colons as the opening fence and nothing else on its line except whitespace. As in CommonMark fences, an opening or closing fence MAY be indented by up to three spaces; a line indented by four or more spaces MUST NOT be a fence. The requirement body MUST be all Markdown between those fence lines, including headings, examples, diagrams, and blank lines, and MUST NOT include text after the closing fence. Leading and trailing whitespace MUST be excluded when comparing the body with evidence.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: `requirements.ci.span` is satisfied.**\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- `colon_fence` (`src/lib.rs:664`, marked `{impl}`) accepts a line only if it has at most three leading spaces and at least three colons. A line indented by four or more spaces is not a fence.\n- `definition_blocks` (`src/lib.rs:520`, marked `{impl}`) checks that the opening fence's suffix starts with `{req}`, then whitespace, then a valid name. `:::{req}name` is rejected.\n- The closing fence is the first later line that meets three conditions:\n  - it is a colon fence;\n  - it has `count >= fence_length`;\n  - nothing but whitespace follows its colons.\n- The closing-fence search skips lines inside Markdown code blocks. That is consistent with the body including examples and diagrams, and with how fences work in CommonMark.\n- The body runs from the line after the opening fence to the start of the closing-fence line. All Markdown in between is kept, including headings, code fences containing `:::`, and blank lines. Nothing after the closing fence is included. A missing closing fence is recorded as `closed: false`.\n- `collect_definitions` (`src/evidence.rs`) computes the text with `without_options(...).trim()`, so leading and trailing whitespace is excluded. That line carries its own `{impl}` marker for `requirements.ci.span`.\n- `without_options` strips `:impl:` and `:test:` option lines from the compared text. This behavior is specified by `requirements.references.anchors`, which these functions also mark `{impl}`.\n- The other code in the `{impl}` functions (name validation, protected ranges, option parsing) is covered by other requirements they are marked as implementing: `ci.identifiers`, `references.markdown_literals`, `ci.fence_lengths` and `references.anchors`. I found no unmarked parser for requirement definitions.\n- Other fence uses — the `{requirements-index}` marker and the book renderer — reuse `colon_fence` and `definition_blocks`. They do not parse requirement spans separately.\n\n**Tests** (all marked `{test}` for `requirements.ci.span`)\n\n- `a_longer_colon_fence_contains_a_shorter_nested_directive` (`src/lib.rs:1258`): a four-colon fence containing a nested `:::` block, with exact body offsets and the text after the closing fence excluded.\n- `closing_fences_allow_no_trailing_text_and_fences_allow_three_spaces_of_indent` (`src/lib.rs:1274`):\n  - `:::foo` does not close the block, while `::: ` with trailing whitespace does;\n  - three-space-indented fences work;\n  - a four-space-indented opener is not a definition, and a four-space-indented closer does not close;\n  - `:::{req}ready` is rejected.\n- `colon_requirement_span_includes_markdown_and_excludes_following_text` (`src/evidence.rs:2364`): the body includes a code fence containing `:::`, an ATX heading and a setext heading. The text after the closing fence is excluded, and a second requirement follows.\n- `requirement_bodies_are_compared_without_surrounding_whitespace` (`src/evidence.rs:2267`): the body is trimmed of surrounding whitespace.\n- `reports_a_missing_closing_colon_fence` (`src/evidence.rs:2397`): an unclosed block produces an error.\n- `validates_full_names_requirement_text_and_input_lines` (`src/evidence.rs:1741`): an end-to-end check with a Markdown body that contains a code block.\n- `validates_four_or_more_colon_fences_and_longer_closers` (`src/evidence.rs:2430`) covers longer closers. It is marked only for `ci.fence_lengths`, not for this requirement.\n\n**Gaps**\n\nI found no unmarked implementation and no unspecified behavior in the marked code. There are no FIXMEs. A fence of `n` colons closed by a longer fence is covered by the `ci.fence_lengths` test and, at equal length, by the nested-directive test, so coverage of boundaries is adequate.",
